<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Funj Sultanate of Sennar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sennar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Funj Sultanate of Sennar was a Muslim state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, founded in the early sixteenth century. It was a neighbour and rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a>, and the two contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a>, before Sennar was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar.jpg" alt="Funj Sultanate of Sennar" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeGabrie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funj_Sultanate_1700.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Christian kingdoms of Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> began to decline from the 12th century. By the 13th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a> seemed to have broken into petty states, and between the 14th and 15th centuries many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> moved in, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Arabic. Tradition names one of them, Abdallah Jammah of the Abdallabi, who created a tribal federation and destroyed what was left of Alodia. In the early 16th century the federation was attacked from the south by the Funj, nomadic pastoralists who followed their own religion. The Funj founded their capital Sennar on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> and ruled a population that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a>, Beja, Bedouin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamaj">Hamaj</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1525 the Ottoman admiral Selman Reis called the kingdom weak and easily conquerable, and noted a yearly tribute of 9,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> paid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The Funj exported horses to Ethiopia, and in 1618 and 1619 war with the Ethiopian emperor Susenyos devastated many eastern provinces. The state reached its peak in the late 17th century, when it stretched from the Third Cataract to the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian Highlands</a>, east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a>. Under Badi III, Sennar was a prosperous and cosmopolitan trading city.</p><p>In the 18th century the monarchy lost power to merchants, scholars and discontented nobles. About 1718 Unsa III was overthrown by the Funj aristocracy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamaj">Hamaj</a> seized power in a coup in 1762. From the late 18th century the sultanate broke up in civil wars. In 1821 the last sultan, greatly weakened, surrendered to the Turco-Egyptian conquest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The sultan, called mek, was chosen by the royal court from the sons of earlier rulers and Funj noblewomen, and a council of 20 elders had a say in decisions. Provinces were governed by a manjil, subdivided among makks, and each manjil had to travel to Sennar yearly to pay tribute. The army was feudal, and most warriors were slaves taken in annual raids on the Nuba mountains. The Funj adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, but its observance among the populace remained thin until the 18th century. Trade in gold, caravans and slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> supplied the sultans' wealth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Funj Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Funj founded <b>Sennar</b> in 1504.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Solong's work was political and religious: he united the Fur and neighbouring groups under a single Keira dynasty and began the Islamisation of the royal court, which placed the sultanate within the wider network of Sahelian Islamic states stretching from Bornu to <b>Sennar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scottish traveller James Bruce passed through <b>Sennar</b> in 1772 on his way back from Ethiopia and left one of the few eyewitness accounts of the Funj court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abdallab descended from Abdallah Jamma, leader of the Qawasma Arabs, who allied with the <b>Funj</b> leader Amara Dunqas to destroy Alodia around 1504.</blockquote><a href="/state/abdallab-sheikhdom">Abdallab Sheikhdom</a></li></ul></section>
